```javascript
function getActiveUsersSortedByName(users) {
  return users
    .filter(user => user.active === true)
    .sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
}
```

**Example:**

```javascript
const users = [
  { name: "Charlie", active: true },
  { name: "alice", active: true },
  { name: "Bob", active: false },
  { name: "Dana", active: true },
];

console.log(getActiveUsersSortedByName(users));
// [
//   { name: "alice", active: true },
//   { name: "Charlie", active: true },
//   { name: "Dana", active: true }
// ]
```

**Notes:**

- `filter` returns a new array, so the original `users` array isn't mutated by the `sort`.
- `localeCompare` gives a proper alphabetical order that's case-insensitive in most locales (so "alice" comes before "Charlie") and handles accented characters well. For explicit control, you can use:
  ```javascript
  a.name.localeCompare(b.name, undefined, { sensitivity: "base" })
  ```
- If `name` might be missing, you could guard against it with `(a.name ?? "").localeCompare(b.name ?? "")`.
- If you want to include truthy values of `active` (not just `true`), change the filter to `user => user.active`.

