Here's a robust function that builds a URL from a base path and a query parameters object:

```javascript
/**
 * Builds a URL with query parameters.
 *
 * @param {string} base - The base URL or path (e.g. "https://api.example.com/users" or "/search").
 * @param {Object} [params={}] - Key/value pairs to use as query parameters.
 * @returns {string} The complete URL.
 */
function buildUrl(base, params = {}) {
  const searchParams = new URLSearchParams();

  for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(params)) {
    // Skip null/undefined values
    if (value === undefined || value === null) continue;

    // Arrays become repeated keys: tags=a&tags=b
    if (Array.isArray(value)) {
      value.forEach(item => {
        if (item !== undefined && item !== null) {
          searchParams.append(key, String(item));
        }
      });
    } else {
      searchParams.append(key, String(value));
    }
  }

  const queryString = searchParams.toString();
  if (!queryString) return base;

  // Handle base URLs that already contain a query string or a hash
  const [beforeHash, hash] = base.split("#");
  const separator = beforeHash.includes("?")
    ? (beforeHash.endsWith("?") || beforeHash.endsWith("&") ? "" : "&")
    : "?";

  return `${beforeHash}${separator}${queryString}${hash ? "#" + hash : ""}`;
}
```

## Usage

```javascript
buildUrl("https://api.example.com/users", { page: 2, limit: 10 });
// "https://api.example.com/users?page=2&limit=10"

buildUrl("/search", { q: "hello world", tags: ["js", "web"] });
// "/search?q=hello+world&tags=js&tags=web"

buildUrl("/items?sort=asc", { page: 1 });
// "/items?sort=asc&page=1"

buildUrl("/items", { filter: null, name: "Zoë & Co" });
// "/items?name=Zo%C3%AB+%26+Co"

buildUrl("/home", {});
// "/home"
```

## Notes

- **Encoding**: `URLSearchParams` automatically encodes special characters (spaces become `+`, `&` becomes `%26`, etc.).
- **Null/undefined**: These values are skipped, so you don't end up with `key=undefined`.
- **Arrays**: Serialized as repeated keys, which is the most widely supported convention.
- **Existing query strings**: If the base already has a `?`, new params are appended with `&`.
- **Hash fragments**: These are preserved at the end of the URL.

